1) Describe your processing pipeline.
The code loads the input image and converts it to grayscale.
It then applies median filtering to reduce noise, enhances image contrast, detects edges using the Canny method, and applies a low-pass filter in the frequency domain. 
Finally, it displays and saves the processed images.

2) Explain how each stage relates to DSP operations.
- Grayscale conversion: Simplifies the image by representing it with intensity values instead of colour channels.
- Median filtering: Uses neighbouring pixel values to remove noise.
- Contrast enhancement: Adjusts intensity levels to improve image visibility.
- Canny edge detection: Uses image gradients to identify boundaries and sharp intensity changes.
- Frequency-domain filtering: Uses FFT and a low-pass filter to reduce high-frequency components, such as noise and fine details.
- Saving results: Stores the processed images for further analysis and comparison.

3) Discuss improvements or limitations.
The processing pipeline improves image quality by reducing noise, enhancing contrast and highlighting edges. 
However, median and low-pass filtering can remove some fine details.
Canny edge detection may also depend on the selected thresholds. 
The results could be improved by adjusting filter parameters, selecting suitable thresholds and testing the pipeline on different images.
